<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SetListArray is offline-first: no account, no sync, nothing uploaded.

  There is exactly ONE <uses-permission> element here, and it is INTERNET. It
  exists for a single feature — offline webpage capture (`src/capture/`), which
  fetches a page the user has explicitly pasted so that it can be read later
  with no signal. Android refuses the socket outright without it: a package is
  put in the `inet` group by the installer only when the manifest asks. There
  is one call site behind it, `src/capture/fetch.rs`, and nothing else in the
  app opens a socket.

  INTERNET is a **normal** permission: granted at install, never prompted for,
  not listed on the app's permission screen, and not revocable. It buys the app
  no access to anything of the user's — no contacts, no files, no location, no
  identifiers. It is the weakest thing in the permission system, but it is a
  <uses-permission> line all the same, so it is written down here rather than
  slipped in.

  Everything else still needs nothing. App storage goes to app-private storage
  (`AndroidApp::internal_data_path()`), which needs no permission and is
  removed with the app; attachment import (card K4) goes through the system
  file picker, which grants access per file without one either. Camera,
  location and external storage are not asked for and should never be. Adding
  a second permission here is a decision, not an oversight —
  `the_android_manifest_asks_only_for_internet` in `src/lib.rs` fails the build
  if one appears.

  `android:allowBackup="false"` is unchanged and unaffected. The permission
  lets the app reach out; allowBackup would let Google's cloud backup reach in
  and copy the library off the device. Those are opposite directions, and only
  one of them is a feature the user asked for.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="dev.lostconnection.setlistarray"
    android:versionCode="1"
    android:versionName="0.1.0">

    <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion="28" android:targetSdkVersion="35" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      `android:extractNativeLibs="false"` is the whole of card K33's second
      finding, and it is about the installed footprint rather than the
      download. Left unset, the platform default for an APK built without AGP
      is to extract: the installer deflates `lib/arm64-v8a/libsetlistarray.so`
      out of the APK into `/data/app/…/lib/arm64/` and keeps the APK too, so
      the phone stores the library twice. K33 measured it on 2026-08-28 — an
      11.5 MiB APK occupying 46.6 MB installed, 35 MB of which was that second
      copy. With this set, the loader maps the library out of the APK where it
      already is and there is no second copy at all.

      It only works if the APK cooperates: the entry must be **stored**, not
      deflated, and page-aligned so it can be mapped. `build-apk.sh` does both
      (`zip -0` and `zipalign -P 16`), and the test
      `the_apk_stores_its_native_library_uncompressed` in `src/lib.rs` holds
      the three of them together — an APK that sets this attribute over a
      compressed library does not install, and the failure is an installer
      error a long way from either edit.
    -->
    <!--
      `android:icon` and `android:roundIcon` are the app's whole presence on a
      home screen, and until 2026-09-09 neither line was here — an app with no
      icon resource gets Android's default green robot, on the launcher, in
      Recents, in the share sheet it had just worked to get into, and in
      Settings.

      Both point at `res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/*.xml`, which are `<adaptive-icon>`
      elements rather than pictures; those files carry the long version of why
      the artwork could not simply be dropped in at the size it was drawn, and
      what the third `<monochrome>` layer is for. The short version is that
      Android shows the central 72dp of a 108dp layer and masks it to a shape
      the app never learns, so everything under `res/mipmap-*dpi/` is generated
      from `assets/icon/` by `scripts/make-icons.py` rather than exported by
      hand.

      The `mipmap-*dpi/ic_launcher.png` and `ic_launcher_round.png` bitmaps in
      that generated set will never be chosen by the resource resolver on a
      device this app runs on: `anydpi` outranks every density qualifier and
      `minSdkVersion` is 28, so the `-v26` XML always wins. They are shipped
      because plenty of things read an APK's icon *without* asking the
      resolver — `aapt2 dump badging`, store tooling, third-party launchers
      that scrape `mipmap-` entries for their icon packs — and what those find
      should be the designed square lockup (`assets/icon/whole.png`) rather
      than nothing at all. 130 KB against a 26 MB APK.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="SetListArray"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:hasCode="true"
        android:extractNativeLibs="false"
        android:allowBackup="false">

        <!--
          NativeActivity subclass from rinch-android: it loads the .so, owns the
          surface, and exposes the platform services (IME, insets) over JNI.

          `android:configChanges` is a list of the configuration changes this
          activity promises to handle *itself*. Everything named here arrives
          as an `onConfigurationChanged` callback — which rinch's Android shell
          turns into `MainEvent::ConfigChanged` and then into the app's
          configuration-change handler — and everything *not* named here is
          handled by Android in the only other way it knows: it destroys the
          activity and builds a new one, from `onCreate`, with a fresh
          `ANativeWindow`, a fresh Rust process state, and a fresh mount.

          `uiMode` is the newest entry, and it is here because of what was
          measured without it. It covers the system dark-mode switch, and it
          was missing for as long as the app had nothing to do with dark mode
          — which made the destroy-and-recreate above not merely acceptable
          but invisible, because the app restarted and read the theme at mount
          the way it always had.

          It was never actually harmless. On the moto g stylus 5G (SDK 33), an
          `adb shell cmd uimode night yes` against this app in the foreground,
          with `uiMode` absent, does this:

            android_activity: Creating: Activity = 0x3176, saved state size = 0
            RustPanic: rinch-android already initialized

          Android tears the activity down and relaunches it **in the same
          process**, so `android_main` is entered a second time, and
          `rinch_android::init` is a `OnceLock` that panics when it is called
          twice. The process id does not change, which is what makes this so
          easy to miss from the outside: nothing crashes in the way a crash
          normally looks. What is left is a live process with a dead main
          loop, an activity that never draws, and the launcher back on screen.
          Flipping the system theme with the app open simply killed it.

          With `uiMode` present, the same three flips (yes / no / yes) keep pid
          and activity instance, log one `ConfigChanged` each, and the app's
          configuration-change handler reads the new value every time — no
          relaunch, no second `Creating: Activity`, no panic.

          So the two halves of this feature are a Rust file and one word in an
          XML attribute, and the XML is the half that fails silently. If the
          theme ever stops following the system while every test still passes,
          look here first.
        -->
        <!--
          `android:launchMode="singleTask"` is the share filter below's other
          half, and it is the half that decides whether this app survives a
          second share.

          The app is one activity. Left at the default (`standard`), Android
          creates a *new instance* of it for every launch, and it creates that
          instance inside whichever task the launch came from — so a share out
          of Chrome builds a second SetListArray activity in Chrome's task.
          `singleTop` is not enough either: it reuses the instance only when
          the instance is already at the top of the *same* task, which the one
          this app is running in never is when the sender is another app.

          A second instance is the failure the `uiMode` note further down
          already describes in detail, arrived at from a different direction. It
          is a second activity in the *same process*, so `android_main` is
          entered a second time, and `rinch_android::init` is a `OnceLock` that
          panics when it is called twice:

            android_activity: Creating: Activity = 0x…, saved state size = 0
            RustPanic: rinch-android already initialized

          The pid does not change, nothing looks like a crash from outside, and
          what is left is a live process with a dead main loop. Sharing to the
          app twice in one session would do it every time.

          `singleTask` says there is at most one instance of this activity on
          the device and it is the root of its own task. A second share is
          therefore routed to `onNewIntent` on the instance already running —
          which is precisely the door `rinch-android`'s intent plumbing was
          built around, and the launch mode its own documentation names — rather
          than to a second `onCreate`. It also means the share sheet brings the
          app the user already had, with the book they were looking at, instead
          of a copy of it filed under somebody else's task in Recents.

          The cost of `singleTask` is the one it is famous for: it will not
          stack a second copy of itself, and Back at its root leaves. Neither
          costs this app anything. There is nothing here that wants two of
          itself, and `NavStore::press_back` already treats a tab root as
          "nothing left to do but exit" for exactly the reason Android does.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name="com.rinch.RinchActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|uiMode">

            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.lib_name"
                android:value="setlistarray" />

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              The share filter: this app in the system's share sheet, for two
              MIME types and no others.

              An `<intent-filter>` here is a claim made to the whole phone, and
              the phone has no way to check it — Android puts an app in a
              chooser because its manifest said so, and the user finds out
              whether that was true by picking it. So the two types listed are
              exactly the two `AttachmentKind` (`src/model.rs`) can store as
              itself: `application/pdf` becomes a `Pdf` attachment through
              `crate::pdf::import`, and `text/plain` becomes either a capture
              (when the text is a URL) or a `Text` attachment (when it is not) —
              see `crate::share::classify` for that fork and why the second
              branch is not a fallback for the first.

              **`image/*` is deliberately absent.** It is the obvious third
              entry — a photograph of a chart on a music stand is a real thing a
              musician has — and this app cannot store one. `AttachmentKind` is
              `Pdf | CapturedPage | Text`; there is no image kind, nothing
              renders one, and `pdf::import` would refuse the bytes as `NotAPdf`
              having already copied them through Java. Declaring `image/*` would
              put SetListArray in the share sheet of every photo on the device
              and then fail in front of the person who picked it, which is worse
              than not being offered.

              **`ACTION_VIEW` is absent for a different reason**, and it is the
              owner's call rather than a technical limit: a `VIEW` filter over
              `http`/`https` would put this app in the "Open with" chooser for
              every link tapped anywhere on the phone. Being a *share* target is
              something the user opts into per share; being a link handler is
              something they then have to opt out of. `ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE` is
              out of scope for the same kind of reason — one shared thing has
              one answer to "which song is this?", and several would need a
              screen that asks the question several times.

              `CATEGORY_DEFAULT` is not decoration: an implicit intent is only
              ever matched against filters that carry it, so without this line
              the filter is invisible to the chooser and the app simply never
              appears. The failure is a share sheet with nothing new in it,
              which looks exactly like a build that did not install.

              `the_android_manifest_offers_itself_as_a_share_target` in
              `src/lib.rs` holds all of the above: that the filter is here at
              all, that it carries the default category, and that the list of
              MIME types is those two and nothing else. A later edit that adds
              `image/*` fails the build rather than the user.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/pdf" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
